sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-15> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Among two-register machines with increment and decrement-jump instructions, all programs of up to four instructions behave repetitively, only two five-instruction programs give nesting, none up to seven instructions is substantially more complicated, and 126 of the 11,019,960,576 eight-instruction programs show complex behavior.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Register machines need eight instructions to show complex behavior";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “Register Machines” (from p. 97), p. 99. Scope: two-register machines with increment and decrement-jump instructions"
}
